  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThrough early next week (Tuesday)...\n\nThrough tomorrow (Thursday): Morning surface and GOES East water \nvapor/RAP analysis indicate ridge centered over the ArkLaTex. \nContinued warmth and potential records into mid October remain \nlikely. Mean ridge around 593-595DM will continue to build in from\nover the northern Gulf into the Gulf Coast states. Dry weather \nand seasonably warm temperatures (highs in 92-96F range and lows \naround 60 degrees in east MS to mid to upper 60s in central to \nnorthwest Delta) will be the norm. High temperatures could be \nnearing records (average is 81). \n\nThere is enough afternoon mixing and drop in afternoon humidity \ninto critical levels (20 to 25 percent range today). Earlier\ncoordination was completed with with local forestry and land \nmanagement assessment vegetation and dead fuels, even winds of \naround 10 mph are concerning for fire spread at this time. Graphic\nwas upgraded earlier to advertise heightened messaging and \nElevated fire danger HWO graphic into the weekend. Deep mixing \nwill allow for minimum afternoon relative humidity levels to dip \n(30 to 40 percent range) into Thursday afternoon, especially given\ndry weather. High pressure will keep winds relatively light. \nThermal ridge over the Gulf Coast states to the east will keep low\nlevel return flow and heat and humidity. Heat indices will peak \nin the low to mid 90s but nothing on the order of dangerous heat \nstress potential. Ridge will flatten and remained centered east \nleading to a drier easterly flow and lower deep moisture than \ncurrently. This should cause any showers to likely remain out of \nthe region.\n\nLate week through early next week (Friday into Tuesday): Fall\nfrontal passage remains likely late week into next weekend. An \nupper low is ejecting into the Four Corners mid to late week is\nprogged to phase with deepening trough axis/upper jet diving into\ncentral Canada and southeast into the Great Lakes. There is good  \nclustering of synoptic evolution. The evolution has trended\ntowards the more phased and amplified meridional and positively\ntilted trough. Stout, dry cold front will drive dewpoints falling\ninto the mid to upper 30s (36-40F). Cool down remains likely this\nweekend. Highs will be seasonably warm Friday (76-91F), with lows\nin the low to mid 60s (60-66F) that morning and lows that night \nmid to upper 40s to low 50s north of Interstate 20 (45-50F) to \nmid to upper 50s (51-54F) to the south. Seasonable and pleasant \nfall temperatures will build areawide into next weekend (highs of \n69-76F Saturday and 72-82F Sunday and lows of 45-49F north of \nInterstate 20 to 50-52F to the south). With continued drought \nstressed vegetation, this front will increase fire danger concerns.\n1035-1040mb surface high building will build east out of the lee \nside of the Rockies through the Mid West, leading to 6-8mb \npressure gradient and resultant gusty gradient wind of 20-25mph\nsustained and wind gusts reaching 30-35mph at times. This will be\na concern and a fire danger graphic/Elevated risk may need a \npotential categorical upgrade. The Storm Prediction Center is \nstill highlighting a relatively rare (for our area) Marginal \nOutlook for fire conditions on Friday with the expected dry cold \nfrontal passage. Ridge axis will build across the area into early\nnext week. Low chances for isolated showers (PoPs around 15%) will\nbe possible across west and southwest MS and portions of \nnortheast LA heading into Monday and again on Tuesday. /DC/NF/CR/",
